IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `spam-act`
IMPORT `spam-civil-action`

-- Spam Control Act 2007, Part 4 -- worked cases.
--
-- The arithmetic in s 14(3) is the whole of the interesting content, so most of
-- what follows is boundary cases on the two caps and on the condition that
-- lifts one of them.

§ `Fixtures`

GIVEN `contravention` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `loss` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `sender` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `secondary` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Claim
DECIDE `a claim` `contravention` `loss` `sender` `secondary` IS
    Claim WITH
        `there is a contravention of section 9 or 11` IS `contravention`
        `the claimant has suffered loss or damage as a direct or indirect result` IS `loss`
        `the defendant is the sender` IS `sender`
        `the defendant has contravened section 12(1)` IS `secondary`

`a recipient out of pocket, suing the sender` MEANS `a claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
`a recipient out of pocket, suing the abettor` MEANS `a claim` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE
`a recipient merely annoyed, suing the sender` MEANS `a claim` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE
`a recipient out of pocket, suing a bystander` MEANS `a claim` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
`a recipient out of pocket, but no contravention of 9 or 11` MEANS `a claim` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE

GIVEN `messages` IS A NUMBER, `loss` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH AN Award
DECIDE `an award over` `messages` `loss` IS
    Award WITH
        `the number of electronic messages mentioned in section 13(1)` IS `messages`
        `the actual loss suffered by the claimant, in dollars` IS `loss`

-- An ordinary individual claimant: a thousand messages, and the loss provable
-- is the cost of an hour sorting them out.
`1,000 messages, $500 of loss` MEANS `an award over` 1000 500

-- The pair that straddles the aggregate cap. One dollar apart.
`100,000 messages, exactly $1,000,000 of loss` MEANS `an award over` 100000 1000000
`100,000 messages, $1,000,001 of loss` MEANS `an award over` 100000 1000001

-- Enough messages to break the cap, and a loss too large for $25 a message to
-- beat.
`50,000 messages, $1,500,000 of loss` MEANS `an award over` 50000 1500000

-- Not enough messages to break the cap, and a loss above it.
`30,000 messages, $1,200,000 of loss` MEANS `an award over` 30000 1200000

-- Enough of both.
`100,000 messages, $1,200,000 of loss` MEANS `an award over` 100000 1200000

-- The exact message count at which lifting the cap starts to be worth anything.
`40,000 messages, $1,000,001 of loss` MEANS `an award over` 40000 1000001
`40,001 messages, $1,000,001 of loss` MEANS `an award over` 40001 1000001

§§ `Secondary conduct`

GIVEN `abetted` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `induced` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `knowingly concerned` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `conspired` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `carries traffic` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A SecondaryConduct
DECIDE `conduct` `abetted` `induced` `knowingly concerned` `conspired` `carries traffic` IS
    SecondaryConduct WITH
        `aided, abetted or procured a contravention of section 9 or 11` IS `abetted`
        `induced a contravention by threats, promises or otherwise` IS `induced`
        `was knowingly concerned in or a party to a contravention` IS `knowingly concerned`
        `conspired with others to effect a contravention` IS `conspired`
        `provides or operates facilities for online services or network access` IS `carries traffic`
        `provides services relating to or connections for the transmission or routing of data` IS FALSE

`a list broker who procured the mailing` MEANS `conduct` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
`a marketer who leaned on the sender` MEANS `conduct` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE
`a bookkeeper who knew what was going on` MEANS `conduct` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE
`a co-conspirator` MEANS `conduct` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE
`an internet access provider that only carried it` MEANS `conduct` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE
`an internet access provider that also conspired` MEANS `conduct` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE
`a passer-by` MEANS `conduct` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

§§ `Statutory damages factors`

GIVEN `wilful` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `loss` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `benefit` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `deterrence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A StatutoryDamagesFactors
DECIDE `factors` `wilful` `loss` `benefit` `deterrence` IS
    StatutoryDamagesFactors WITH
        `the contravention was wilful` IS `wilful`
        `the claimant has suffered or is likely to suffer loss or damage` IS `loss`
        `a benefit is shown to have accrued to the defendant` IS `benefit`
        `there is a need to deter other similar instances` IS `deterrence`

`a deliberate and profitable campaign` MEANS `factors` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
`an inherited list, no profit shown` MEANS `factors` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE
`nothing aggravating at all` MEANS `factors` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

§ `Section 12 -- aiding, abetting, and the safe harbour`

#ASSERT `a limb of section 12(1) is made out` `a list broker who procured the mailing`
#ASSERT `a limb of section 12(1) is made out` `a marketer who leaned on the sender`
#ASSERT `a limb of section 12(1) is made out` `a bookkeeper who knew what was going on`
#ASSERT `a limb of section 12(1) is made out` `a co-conspirator`
#ASSERT NOT `a limb of section 12(1) is made out` `a passer-by`
#ASSERT NOT `a limb of section 12(1) is made out` `an internet access provider that only carried it`

-- s 12(2) covers the carrier for carrying.
#ASSERT `the person is within the section 12(2) safe harbour` `an internet access provider that only carried it`
#ASSERT NOT `the person is within the section 12(2) safe harbour` `a list broker who procured the mailing`

-- And the words "merely because" mean the carrier who also conspires is both
-- within the safe harbour's description and outside its protection. This
-- encoding reads a made-out limb as taking the person outside "merely because";
-- the pair is asserted so that the choice is visible.
#ASSERT `the person is within the section 12(2) safe harbour` `an internet access provider that also conspired`
#ASSERT `the person contravenes section 12(1)` `an internet access provider that also conspired`
#ASSERT NOT `the person contravenes section 12(1)` `an internet access provider that only carried it`

§ `Section 13 -- standing`

#ASSERT `an action under section 13(1) lies` `a recipient out of pocket, suing the sender`
#ASSERT `an action under section 13(1) lies` `a recipient out of pocket, suing the abettor`

-- Loss or damage is the gate. Being spammed is not loss.
#ASSERT NOT `an action under section 13(1) lies` `a recipient merely annoyed, suing the sender`

-- The defendant must be the sender or a person who contravened s 12(1), and
-- nobody else.
#ASSERT NOT `an action under section 13(1) lies` `a recipient out of pocket, suing a bystander`

-- And there must be a primary contravention of s 9 or s 11. A contravention of
-- s 12(1) alone cannot exist, because s 12(1) is defined by reference to one.
#ASSERT NOT `an action under section 13(1) lies` `a recipient out of pocket, but no contravention of 9 or 11`

-- s 13(2): nothing in this Act says anything about liability elsewhere.
#ASSERT NOT `this Act is the last word on the defendant's liability` `a recipient out of pocket, suing a bystander`
#ASSERT NOT `this Act is the last word on the defendant's liability` `a recipient merely annoyed, suing the sender`

§ `Section 14 -- the ceilings`

-- s 14(3)(b)(i): $25 a message.
#ASSERT `the per-message ceiling` `1,000 messages, $500 of loss` EQUALS 25000
#ASSERT `the per-message ceiling` `100,000 messages, exactly $1,000,000 of loss` EQUALS 2500000
#ASSERT `the per-message ceiling` `40,000 messages, $1,000,001 of loss` EQUALS 1000000

-- The ordinary individual claim: $25 a message comes to fifty times the loss
-- that could be proved, and the aggregate cap is nowhere near.
#ASSERT `the most the claimant may have as statutory damages` `1,000 messages, $500 of loss` EQUALS 25000
#ASSERT `the most the claimant may have as damages` `1,000 messages, $500 of loss` EQUALS 500
#ASSERT `statutory damages are the better election` `1,000 messages, $500 of loss`
#ASSERT `the most the claimant may recover on the better election` `1,000 messages, $500 of loss` EQUALS 25000

-- THE CLIFF. s 14(3)(b)(ii) caps statutory damages at $1 million "unless the
-- claimant proves that the actual loss ... EXCEEDS $1 million". A loss of
-- exactly $1 million does not exceed it.
#ASSERT NOT `the aggregate cap is lifted` `100,000 messages, exactly $1,000,000 of loss`
#ASSERT `the aggregate cap is lifted` `100,000 messages, $1,000,001 of loss`

#ASSERT `the most the claimant may have as statutory damages` `100,000 messages, exactly $1,000,000 of loss` EQUALS 1000000
#ASSERT `the most the claimant may have as statutory damages` `100,000 messages, $1,000,001 of loss` EQUALS 2500000

-- One dollar of further proved loss raises the ceiling by one and a half
-- million. Both claimants are in exactly the same position otherwise.
#ASSERT `what the ceiling would be if the loss were one dollar higher` `100,000 messages, exactly $1,000,000 of loss` EQUALS 2500000
#ASSERT `what one more dollar of proved loss adds to the ceiling` `100,000 messages, exactly $1,000,000 of loss` EQUALS 1500000

-- And nothing at all for the claimant who is already above the cap.
#ASSERT `what one more dollar of proved loss adds to the ceiling` `100,000 messages, $1,000,001 of loss` EQUALS 0

-- At exactly $1 million of loss the two elections are worth the same, so the
-- election is indifferent. At $1,000,001 statutory damages are worth two and a
-- half times the alternative.
#ASSERT NOT `statutory damages are the better election` `100,000 messages, exactly $1,000,000 of loss`
#ASSERT `the most the claimant may recover on the better election` `100,000 messages, exactly $1,000,000 of loss` EQUALS 1000000
#ASSERT `statutory damages are the better election` `100,000 messages, $1,000,001 of loss`
#ASSERT `the most the claimant may recover on the better election` `100,000 messages, $1,000,001 of loss` EQUALS 2500000

§ `Section 14 -- how narrow the escape from the cap is`

-- The escape is conditioned on proving the very thing that makes the other
-- election worth having. These four cases are the whole of the interaction.

-- (1) Cap lifted, but too few messages for $25 each to reach $1 million. The
-- lifting gains nothing: the claimant does better on actual damages.
#ASSERT `the aggregate cap is lifted` `30,000 messages, $1,200,000 of loss`
#ASSERT `the most the claimant may have as statutory damages` `30,000 messages, $1,200,000 of loss` EQUALS 750000
#ASSERT NOT `lifting the aggregate cap is worth anything to this claimant` `30,000 messages, $1,200,000 of loss`
#ASSERT NOT `statutory damages are the better election` `30,000 messages, $1,200,000 of loss`
#ASSERT `the most the claimant may recover on the better election` `30,000 messages, $1,200,000 of loss` EQUALS 1200000

-- (2) Cap lifted, enough messages to pass $1 million, but the loss proved is
-- larger still. Again the claimant elects damages and the lifting is worth
-- nothing.
#ASSERT `the most the claimant may have as statutory damages` `50,000 messages, $1,500,000 of loss` EQUALS 1250000
#ASSERT NOT `lifting the aggregate cap is worth anything to this claimant` `50,000 messages, $1,500,000 of loss`
#ASSERT NOT `statutory damages are the better election` `50,000 messages, $1,500,000 of loss`

-- (3) Cap lifted, and $25 a message beats the loss. This is the only case in
-- which s 14(3)(b)(ii)'s proviso does any work.
#ASSERT `lifting the aggregate cap is worth anything to this claimant` `100,000 messages, $1,200,000 of loss`
#ASSERT `statutory damages are the better election` `100,000 messages, $1,200,000 of loss`
#ASSERT `the most the claimant may recover on the better election` `100,000 messages, $1,200,000 of loss` EQUALS 2500000

-- (4) The boundary in the message count. $1,000,000 divided by $25 is 40,000, so
-- at 40,000 messages the per-message limb lands exactly on the aggregate cap and
-- lifting the cap is worth nothing; at 40,001 it is worth $25.
#ASSERT `the most the claimant may have as statutory damages` `40,000 messages, $1,000,001 of loss` EQUALS 1000000
#ASSERT NOT `lifting the aggregate cap is worth anything to this claimant` `40,000 messages, $1,000,001 of loss`
#ASSERT `the most the claimant may have as statutory damages` `40,001 messages, $1,000,001 of loss` EQUALS 1000025
#ASSERT `lifting the aggregate cap is worth anything to this claimant` `40,001 messages, $1,000,001 of loss`

§ `Section 14(4) -- factors, not elements`

#ASSERT `how many of the four specific factors in section 14(4) are present` `a deliberate and profitable campaign` EQUALS 4
#ASSERT `how many of the four specific factors in section 14(4) are present` `an inherited list, no profit shown` EQUALS 2
#ASSERT `how many of the four specific factors in section 14(4) are present` `nothing aggravating at all` EQUALS 0

-- Wilfulness appears in s 14(4)(a) and nowhere in s 9 or s 11. It bears on what
-- the contravention costs and not on whether there was one.
#ASSERT NOT `wilfulness is required for the contravention` `nothing aggravating at all`
#ASSERT NOT `wilfulness is required for the contravention` `a deliberate and profitable campaign`

§ `Section 15 -- costs`

#ASSERT `the court may order the defendant to pay the claimant's costs under section 15` `a recipient out of pocket, suing the sender`
#ASSERT NOT `the court may order the defendant to pay the claimant's costs under section 15` `a recipient merely annoyed, suing the sender`

-- s 15 names the defendant as payer and nobody else.
#ASSERT NOT `section 15 lets the court order the claimant to pay the defendant's costs` `a recipient out of pocket, suing the sender`
#ASSERT NOT `section 15 lets the court order the claimant to pay the defendant's costs` `a recipient out of pocket, suing a bystander`
